<!-- v3 (2026-11-17, help overhaul): John's structure (intro, What it does,
     How to use this tool, then more) and the shared blocks in ui/css/help.css. -->
<p>Sometimes YouTube is the only place a recording can be found.</p>

<h3>What it does:</h3>
<p>YouTube to MP3 turns a YouTube link into MP3s, one per song, saved in an album folder and ready for <strong class="tool">Unpack &amp; Convert</strong>. It handles three kinds of link:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>A song:</strong> one video becomes one MP3.</li>
    <li><strong>A playlist</strong> (the link contains <code>list=</code>): each video becomes its own MP3, numbered in playlist order.</li>
    <li><strong>A whole album in one video:</strong> MetaForge works out where each song starts and cuts the video into one MP3 per song.</li>
</ul>

<section class="steps">
    <h3>How to use this tool:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Make and name the album's folder first (the folder picker has a <strong>New folder</strong> button). The tool never makes a folder of its own, so your folder names stay consistent.</li>
        <li>Paste the link into <strong>YouTube link</strong> and choose <span class="ui-button">Look up</span>.</li>
        <li>Check the details: <strong>Artist</strong>, <strong>Album</strong>, and the <strong>Album folder</strong> (<span class="ui-button">Choose…</span>).
            <ul>
                <li>For a single video, <strong>This video is</strong> lets you say whether it's <strong>One song</strong> or <strong>A whole album</strong>.</li>
                <li>For a playlist, deselect any <strong>Songs</strong> that don't belong.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Choose <span class="ui-button">Make the MP3</span> (for a playlist, <span class="ui-button">Make 12 MP3s</span>, or however many are selected).</li>
        <li>When it's done, <span class="ui-button">Continue to Unpack &amp; Convert</span> carries the folder, artist and album over.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">If the album folder already has MP3s in it, nothing is made, so two albums never mix.</p>
</section>

<h3>A whole album in one video</h3>
<ol>
    <li>Choose <strong>A whole album: make one MP3 per song</strong>, check the <strong>Track titles, one per line</strong>, in order, then choose <span class="ui-button">Find the tracks</span>.</li>
    <li>MetaForge downloads the audio and works out where each song starts. It uses the video's chapters, or the times in its description, if it has them. If not, it listens for the quiet gaps between songs, guided by the track lengths MusicBrainz has for the album.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the cuts:</strong> every start time can be played (a few seconds either side) and corrected before anything is made. Cuts marked <em>Check</em> are the ones to listen to. <span class="ui-button">Change the track list</span> goes back a step.</li>
    <li>Choose <span class="ui-button">Make 10 MP3s</span> (the number of tracks).</li>
</ol>

<h3>Sound quality</h3>
<p>YouTube's best audio (about 130 kbps) is converted once to MP3 V0, the same as the rest of MetaForge. It can't be better than YouTube's copy.</p>
<p>Unpack &amp; Convert then adds cover art, sets the filing category and writes the album details that <strong class="tool">MusicBrainz IDs</strong> needs. It only renames these MP3s, so there's no further loss of quality. The one exception: <strong>Apply Loudness Normalization</strong> re-encodes them.</p>

<p class="note">YouTube changes often. If links stop working, yt-dlp (in the <code>bin</code> folder) needs updating.</p>

<p>See also: <button type="button" class="see-also" data-mf-help="unpack_convert" data-mf-help-name="Unpack &amp; Convert">Unpack &amp; Convert</button>, the next step.</p>
<!-- --- END OF FILE help.mfi --- -->
